WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Instructor] Let's say
that you have a solution

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.070
that where the solvent is water

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.734
and the solute is what

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:12.420
we would consider drinking
alcohol or ethanol.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.830
So this is our solution right over here.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:19.493
And let's say that it is 10% ethanol,

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.590
which is drinking alcohol.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.590
And let's say the rest of it is water.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.410
And what we wanna do is

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.170
figure out a way that we can get something

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.450
that has 15% or 20 or 30% ethanol.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.863
How would we do it?

00:00:35.863 --> 00:00:38.989
Well, the answer lies
in the fact that ethanol

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:42.150
and water have different boiling points.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.870
For example, the boiling point of ethanol

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.830
is approximately 78 degrees Celsius.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.390
While the boiling point of water

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.240
is 100 degrees Celsius.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.090
And so a mixture of ethanol

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.560
and water is going to have

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.270
a boiling point someplace in between.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.680
And so you could imagine

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.850
we can keep heating up this solution.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.150
We're going to go beyond

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.830
the boiling point of ethanol.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.240
But before we get to the
boiling point of water,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.780
this solution is going to boil.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.320
And when it's boiling,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.250
because ethanol has a lower boiling point,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.080
the vapor mixture is going to contain

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.040
more than 10% ethanol.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.990
And if you could condense that vapor,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.626
you then have a solution

00:01:29.626 --> 00:01:32.290
with higher than 10% ethanol.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:37.220
And that is exactly what
distillation is all about.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.820
So you might distill with a contraption

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:40.653
that looks like this.

00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:42.200
At first, it looks complicated,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.830
but then when you really
understand what's going on,

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:45.180
it's actually pretty straightforward.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.430
It's exactly what I just described.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.730
So what we would do is

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.390
we would put the original
solution right over here,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.790
and then you would heat it up.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.840
Oftentimes you're gonna
heat it up in an oil bath.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.673
That's what you see

00:01:57.673 --> 00:01:58.900
in this yellow right over here,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.240
because the oil won't evaporate readily

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.910
and it can distribute the heat

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.740
around in a nice even way.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.630
And then if you were to heat this up

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.230
so that the solution boils,

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.700
remember that's going
to be a boiling point

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.130
someplace in between 78 degrees Celsius

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:13.680
and a 100 degrees Celsius.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:14.640
You're going to for sure

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.428
have some water molecules vaporizing,

00:02:17.428 --> 00:02:18.700
but at that temperature,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.970
which is beyond ethanol's boiling point,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.060
you're for sure we're going to have

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.400
a higher proportion of
ethanol molecules vaporizing,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.090
and then they're going to be,

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.310
they're going to be in vapor form.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.280
And so they could enter
this chamber right here.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.880
And what this chamber is,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.350
is essentially what's called a Condenser.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.140
So oftentimes you're going to have

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.880
separate from the vapor,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.000
but you're going to
make water, cold water,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.150
go around this tube right over here,

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.530
oftentimes a glass tube.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.150
Sometimes you might chill
it with cold air instead,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.460
but you have water coming in, cold water,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.510
and it's going around the tube.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.430
It's not mixing with the
actual air inside the tube.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.050
And then the water comes out of the tube.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:57.890
But what it does is it lowers

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.740
the temperature in that tube.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.860
And so you can imagine the vapor

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.100
that comes in that contains

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.530
both ethanol and water,

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:04.840
but it's a proportion of the vapor

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.850
that is ethanol is going to be higher

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.280
than the proportion in
our original solution.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.490
It's going to condense
back into liquid form.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.576
And then that stuff is just going

00:03:12.576 --> 00:03:14.088
to pour back down into

00:03:14.088 --> 00:03:16.910
what you could call this
collection chamber right here.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.380
And the collection chamber

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.528
is surrounded by a very cold,

00:03:20.528 --> 00:03:25.470
oftentimes ice bath so that
you minimize vaporization.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.040
Depending on how well you do this,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.560
maybe this sample right over here

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.870
might be 20% ethanol

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:31.200
or 30% ethanol.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.710
There will be still some water

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.760
that comes up here and condenses,

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.680
and then comes back down.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.050
Now, if you wanted an even
pure sample of ethanol,

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.080
you could keep doing this.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:42.770
You could take what you collect here

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.010
and then heat that up

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.650
to its boiling point,

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.430
which will now be a little bit lower

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.600
than the first solution,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.640
because it has a higher
proportion of ethanol.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.680
And then you would have

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.040
the same process go over

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:53.873
and over again.

00:03:53.873 --> 00:03:55.340
And so every time you do that,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.480
you would get a pure

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.710
and pure sample of ethanol.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.230
If you don't wanna do that,

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.300
'cause it's tiring.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.170
It takes a lot of work.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.060
And actually you end up with

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.370
a lot of lost material

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.490
through all of this mechanism over here.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:11.430
There's something you can do
called Fractional Distillation.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.270
And what fractional distillation is,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:15.220
is getting multiple vaporization

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.430
and condensation cycles all at once

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.250
so that you get a more pure sample.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.130
And the way that you get

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.280
those multiple vaporization
in condensation cycles

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.307
is to give the vapor in
this tube right over here,

00:04:27.307 --> 00:04:29.850
something to condense on

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:30.930
and that condensates,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.690
some of it will revaporize.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.850
And some of it will drip back down.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.540
And the part that revaporizes

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.150
is likely to have a higher
proportion of the distillate

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.690
of the thing that has
the lower boiling point.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.650
And you can have multiple
cycles of this condensation

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.790
and revaporization inside this column.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:49.970
And so at the end,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.280
you're more likely to have

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.723
a more pure sample of your distillate.

